<<<md>>>
# The MRI System

**Part:** B — Hardware · **Reading time:** ~50 min · **Pass 1 overview:** [The MRI Scanner](../pass1_big_picture/P1.2_The_MRI_Scanner.ipynb) · **Refreshers you may want:** [Magnetism from currents](../refreshers/RP1_Magnetism_from_Currents.ipynb), [Induction and RF](../refreshers/RP2_Induction_and_RF.ipynb), [Continuous FT and DFT](../refreshers/RM3_Continuous_FT_and_DFT.ipynb)

Every MRI system consists of several major components that are required to create a measurable magnetic resonance signal and to localize where in the body it came from. In this chapter we "cut open" a scanner and look at what is inside, *why* each part is there, and how big the numbers are (fields, frequencies, powers, voltages, costs). This is the **map** of the hardware part of the course: each subsystem is covered in depth in its own chapter ([B2 fields](B2_Magnetic_Fields_in_MRI.ipynb), [B3 main magnet](B3_Main_Magnet_Engineering.ipynb), [B4 gradients](B4_Gradient_System_Engineering.ipynb), [B5 RF chain](B5_RF_System_and_Receive_Chain.ipynb), [B6 safety](B6_MRI_Safety.ipynb)).

<<<md>>>
## Learning goals

1. Describe the fundamental hardware components of an MRI scanner and why they are necessary
    * What are the required components of an MRI system?
    * What is the purpose of each component, and in which step of the MRI experiment is it used?
    * What calibrations are required for an MRI scan (at installation and before every scan)?
    * Answer these questions:
        * Why is MRI so loud?
        * Why is it dangerous to bring certain metals into an MRI scanner?
2. *(new)* Know the supporting subsystems that a real scanner cannot work without: shim coils, spectrometer/sequencer, RF power amplifier, preamplifiers and ADCs, reconstruction computer, Faraday cage, patient table, cooling and cryogenics.
3. *(new)* Quote the order of magnitude of every field, frequency, power and signal level in the system.
4. *(new)* Explain the trade-offs between clinical field strengths (0.55 T, 1.5 T, 3 T, 7 T), open vs closed bores, and what drives the cost of a scanner.

<<<md>>>
## Symbols used in this chapter

| Symbol | Meaning (plain words) | SI unit | Typical value / remark |
|---|---|---|---|
| $x, y, z$ | position coordinates; $z$ along the bore (the $B_0$ direction) | m | |
| $\vec r = [x,y,z]^T$ | position vector | m | |
| $t$ | time | s | |
| $B_0$ | main static magnetic field (along $z$) | T | 0.55, 1.5, 3, 7 T |
| $\Delta B_0(\vec r)$ | deviation of the static field from $B_0$ | T | quoted in ppm of $B_0$ |
| ppm | "parts per million" = $10^{-6}$ (relative) | – | 1 ppm of 3 T = 3 µT |
| $B_1^+(\vec r,t)$ | transmit RF field (the rotating component that tips spins) | T | ~1–25 µT |
| $B_1^-(\vec r)$ | receive sensitivity of an RF coil (field per unit current) | T/A | ~µT/A |
| $\vec G(t)=[G_x,G_y,G_z]^T$ | gradient: rate of change of $B_z$ with position | T/m | 20–80 mT/m |
| $SR$ | slew rate = maximum $dG/dt$ | T/m/s | 100–200 T/m/s |
| $\gamma$ | gyromagnetic ratio (angular) | rad s⁻¹ T⁻¹ | $2.675\times10^8$ for ¹H |
| $\bar\gamma=\gamma/2\pi$ | gyromagnetic ratio ("gamma-bar") | Hz/T | 42.58 MHz/T for ¹H |
| $f_0=\bar\gamma B_0$ | Larmor (resonance) frequency | Hz | 63.9 MHz @ 1.5 T, 127.7 MHz @ 3 T |
| $\Delta f$ | frequency offset from $f_0$ | Hz | |
| $\theta$ | flip angle | rad (or °) | 90° = $\pi/2$ |
| $T_{rf}$ | RF pulse duration | s | 0.1–10 ms |
| $\vec M$, $M_0$ | net magnetization; its equilibrium value | A/m (normalized $M_0=1$ in simulations) | ~0.01 A/m for water at 3 T |
| $s(t)$ | received signal | V | µV–mV at the coil |
| $\sigma_n$ | standard deviation of the noise | V (or a.u.) | |
| $SNR$ | signal-to-noise ratio | – | |
| $g_{rx}$ | receiver gain (amplification before the ADC) — not the parallel-imaging g-factor | – (or dB) | |
| $b$ | number of bits of the analog-to-digital converter (ADC) | – | 14–18 |
| $I_c$ | electric current (in a wire or coil) | A | gradient coils: several hundred A |
| $\ell$ | length of a wire segment | m | |
| $F$ | force | N | |
| $\mu_0$ | vacuum permeability | T m/A | $4\pi\times10^{-7}$ |
| $c$ | speed of light | m/s | $3.0\times10^8$ |
| $\lambda$ | wavelength of the RF field | m | 2.35 m in air at 127.7 MHz |
| $\varepsilon_r$ | relative permittivity of tissue | – | ~50–80 (water-rich tissue) |
| $f_c$, $d$ | cutoff frequency and diameter of a waveguide (Faraday-cage penetration) | Hz, m | |
| $FOV$ | field of view | m | 0.2–0.5 m |

> **⚠️ Common confusion:** **T** (upright) is the unit *tesla*; $T$ (italic, no subscript) is temperature; $T_1, T_2$ are relaxation time constants. $I$ is the spin quantum number in physics chapters — in this hardware chapter we write currents as $I_c$ to avoid the clash.

<<<code>>>
import sys, os
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.abspath(".."))   # make ../mri_tools.py importable
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import mri_tools as mt
mt.setup_plots()
